      "lineNbr": 60,
      "author": {
        "id": 1937676
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-06T04:38:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The current implementation in crosvm guarantees a 1:1 mapping with vCPU-to-pCPU pinning and vCPU thread migration is not supported in the \"--host-cpu-topology\" configuration. It\u0027s done intentionally this way to let the guest scheduler make task placement decisions. While it\u0027s true that the same cpus can share a frequency domain in the cluster, this is currently not supported and has different implications on the virt cpufreq device backend and the host scheduler. Being individually scalable has its advantages, like communicating to the host about the requirements of a single vCPU thread as opposed to scaling the entire cluster(or a collection vCPU threads in this case), which would allow the host to make better task placement decisions. So I\u0027m okay with this more restrictive assumption until/if we decide to introduce clustering support(note that even if you don\u0027t point to the same exact opptable, cpufreq still has ways to allow different cpus to share the freq domain if they share a common freq plan).",
      "parentUuid": "758d3e33_0214b98a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 60,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 60,
        "endChar": 39
      },
      "revId": "5306457eca6defbbadab16c726424d6ac4a7918a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
